  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAfter some patchy frost across the north this morning,\ntemperatures will recover into the 60s as high pressure remains in\ncontrol of the weather across the western Great Lakes. This high\nwill mean a persistence forecast will rule through the early part\nof the weekend with continued dry conditions expected.\n\nEnsembles then continue to bring marginal rain chances to \nnortheast Wisconsin later this weekend as a surface low spins up \nover the Intermountain West and ejects north into Canada. However,\ncurrent thinking is that high pressure will win the battle as it \nso often does in this scenario, especially with no veritable \nmoisture transport and lack of low-level forcing mechanism. This\nhas already started to come to fruition as the main window of\nprecipitation has shifted later to a window from Saturday\nafternoon through early Sunday morning. Have retained 20 to 40% \nPoPs over our western counties where confidence is higher in at \nleast light rain occurring before scattering out further east.\nAfter this initial shot of possible rain this weekend, another dry\nperiod is then expected from Sunday afternoon through the early\npart of next week.\n\nBetter chances for more widespread rainfall look to be during the\nmiddle of next week as high pressure breaks down and deepening \nlow pressure tracks through the upper Midwest. However, trends \nwill have to be monitored as ensembles still diverge on the \noverall synoptic pattern. Otherwise, most of the extended will be \ncharacterized by temperatures mainly in the 60s, warming into the \n70s by early next week.",
